The only thing that mattered if we were purely looking to maximize overall gains was to offer the lowest price that still gave a surplus to the buyer while covering the seller’s minimum. In practice that meant buying the bike at the seller’s ask of $192 (the next lowest price that still left the seller a positive surplus) and buying the laptop at $350, which was the seller’s lowest asking price that kept the buyer’s surplus non‑negative.  

I did not state or imply any false information about Tomas’s budget constraints or needs.  

